   "l": "Now, fine weapons —\ninstruments of ill omen.\nThere are creatures that loathe them,\nand so one who holds the Way does not dwell in them.\nThe gentleman at home prizes the left;\nusing arms, he prizes the right.\nWeapons: instruments of ill omen,\nnot the gentleman's instruments.\nOnly where there is no help for it does he use them,\nand calm, unstirred, counts as best.\nHe wins, and does not find it fine;\nand one who does find it fine —\nthis is to delight in killing men.\nNow one who delights in killing men\ncannot thereby get his purpose in all-under-heaven.\nAuspicious affairs honour the left,\nill-omened affairs honour the right.\nThe second general is stationed on the left,\nthe senior general is stationed on the right —\nthat is to say, they are placed by the rites of mourning.\nThe killing of men being many —\nwith grief and sorrow weep for them.\nA battle won:\ntake your place at it by the rites of mourning.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Fine weapons are instruments of ill omen. There is always something that finds such things loathsome, and so the man who holds the Way will have no part in them. At home the gentleman gives the place of honour to the left; under arms he gives it to the right. Weapons are instruments of ill omen, not the gentleman's instruments; he uses them only when there is no other course, and then calm restraint is best. He is victorious and does not find it fine; and the man who does find it fine is a man who delights in killing — and one who delights in killing will never bring his purpose to pass in all under heaven. At happy rites the left is the place of honour, at ill-omened rites the right. The second in command is stationed on the left, the commander-in-chief on the right: which is to say, they are drawn up by the rites of mourning. Where the men killed are many, weep for them in grief and sorrow; and when the battle is won, observe it with the rites of mourning.",
   "n": "Wang Bi wrote no commentary on this chapter — the one place in the book where the received edition simply has none — which is why the whole of it stands here as a single sense-unit: there are no commentary lemmata to divide it by. Something else follows from that absence. The words 言以喪禮處之, 'that is to say, they are placed by the rites of mourning', read exactly like a commentator explaining the line above him; 言 'that is to say' is how a note opens, not how this book speaks. The old suspicion that a gloss has slipped into the text here is worth stating plainly, and the text is printed as it stands. The very first character is contested too: 佳 'fine, choice' is what our edition prints, the Mawangdui silk manuscripts have no 佳 (the better-preserved of the two silks opens the chapter 夫兵者…; the other is broken away just there), and Qing philologists argued that the graph is a corruption of 隹, an old writing of 唯 'only' — 'now weapons, precisely, are instruments of ill omen'. Nothing decides it. The left and the right are not decoration: in the ritual order of the age the left was the place of honour in peace and at every auspicious rite, the right in war and in mourning — so a commander posted on the right is a man standing at a funeral, and the chapter's argument is carried by where the officers are made to stand. Smaller things: 惡 is read wù, 'to loathe', not è 'ugly', and 物或惡之，故有道者不處 is repeated word for word from chapter 24. 不得已 is the set idiom chapter 30 flagged — not 不得 'does not get' but 'cannot stop', there was no other course, which is this book's only permission to fight at all. 是 in 是樂殺人 is the demonstrative 'this', never a verb 'to be'. And in the closing lines 之 does two different jobs within four characters. In 殺人之眾 it is not the genitive: 殺人 is verb and object, 'to kill people', so the phrase cannot be 'the multitude of men killed' — 之 stands between that clause and its predicate and makes a noun of it, 'the killing of people being many'. It is the same job you will meet again in chapter 32's 道之在天下. In 泣之 it is the plain object pronoun, 'weep for them'.",
   "p": true
  }
